#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9357cc9-e383-5d88-9741-f49f6fbcca99" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "8e2f5bec-3573-5f1d-9870-41ac26b1ddf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13342" :question/problem "| $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n| $25$ | $20$ |\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}4}$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{4 \\cdot 4} &= \\frac{20}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}16}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}25}$ | ${\\color{red}20}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}16$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60021495-9d2a-58a5-9321-bb16013bbba7" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying and Dividing"}] :question/id #uuid "11fac5c9-ca4a-54a6-9f38-3c675411673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13343" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $6$ | $18$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $24$ |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\div 6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\div 6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}$\n- To create a numerator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}6}$ | ${\\color{red}18}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ | ${\\color{red}24}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$ |\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e3287b0-2ecb-5d83-9596-7943d68f8884" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Numerators"}] :question/id #uuid "e2ada4db-c1f0-53b0-9af7-ee2712ae75ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-13409" :question/problem "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $10$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $35$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $50$ |" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}5}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}3}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}5}$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $10$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}}{\\color{red}10}$\n- To create a denominator of $35$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $7{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 7}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 7} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}}{\\color{red}35}$\n- To create a denominator of $50$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $10{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 10}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 10} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}}{\\color{red}50}$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}3}$ | ${\\color{red}5}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}$ | ${\\color{red}35}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}$ | ${\\color{red}50}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84a526bf-9d98-54e1-a54e-cd625bbaea49" :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "f1a6757d-3e68-5263-a6c7-d03c73391848" :question/math-academy-id "e-13410" :question/problem "Harvey's parents reward him for each hour he spends helping with the housework. The following ratio table shows the number of hours of work Harvey did over the last three weeks and the corresponding rewards.\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{1 \\cdot 3} &= \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5}{1 \\cdot 5} &= \\frac{15}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$ |\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5$."}]}
